# Build output & caches. Note `dist-host/` and `dist-perf/` are deliberately NOT listed here:
# prettier reads the root `.gitignore` too, which already covers both, and adding them would
# be redundant rather than defensive. What that does NOT extend to is a NESTED .gitignore —
# prettier reads the root one only — which is exactly why the two `apps/mobile` rules further
# down are load-bearing even though Capacitor ignores those paths in its own ignore files.
dist/
**/dist/
.turbo/
coverage/
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Not ours to format
LICENSE

# The generated native project trees (#135). Everything prettier can even PARSE inside them
# is machine-written, and formatting any of it is actively wrong:
#   - `*/public/**` and `capacitor.config.json` are the synced web payload and its generated
#     config — build output that `cap sync` overwrites, and gitignored by Capacitor's own
#     ignore files;
#   - `Assets.xcassets/**/Contents.json` is the Xcode asset catalog's index, rewritten
#     verbatim by `cap add` and `@capacitor/assets` — reformatting it would put the tracked
#     file permanently out of sync with what regeneration produces.
# Everything else in there (Swift, Kotlin/Java, Gradle, plists, xcconfig, storyboards) has no
# prettier parser at all, so a narrower rule would buy nothing and would go red again the
# next time the templates gain a JSON file. Derived from the eleven files `format:check`
# actually reported after the first `cap add` + `cap sync`, not guessed.
apps/mobile/ios/
apps/mobile/android/

# Machine-generated (byte-identical to scripts/i18n-gen.mjs output; the i18n:check
# gate verifies it, so prettier must not reformat it out of sync)
**/i18n/catalog.gen.ts

